# ONE ORIGIN, TWO APPS.
#
# The browser bundle asks for `/backend/*` and everything else goes to Next.
# That is not a deployment preference — it is what makes a PUBLISHED image
# possible at all. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is substituted into the client bundle at
# build time, so an image built with an absolute API URL works on exactly one
# hostname; every other user's browser calls their own machine. A relative
# `/backend` resolves against whatever host served the page, and one image
# then serves everybody.
#
# Which means this proxy is not optional and does not belong in an overlay:
# without it the bundle's default has nothing to resolve against, and the
# README quickstart 404s on every page while the API is perfectly healthy.
#
# NextAuth's own `/api/auth/*` lives in the Next app, so only the `/backend`
# prefix is peeled off — the two `/api` namespaces never meet.
:80 {
	handle_path /backend/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}
